    "lawCode": "Amd §§1118 & 1115, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3387\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to\nexemptions from the sales and compensating use tax for tastings held\nby a licensed brewery, farm brewery, cider producer, farm cidery,\ndistillery or farm distillery in accordance with the alcoholic\nbeverage control law\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis legislation would amend the tax law to provide wine, beer, cider,\nand liquor tastings by a licensed producer to be exempt from the sales\nand compensating use tax.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Amends subdivision 14 of section 1118 of the tax law, to\ninclude any charge for beer, cider, or liquor tastings to be use tax\nexempt.\n\nSection 2. Amends paragraph 33 of subdivision (a) Section 1115 of the\nTax Law, to include any charge for beer, cider, or liquor tastings to\nbe sales tax exempt.\n\nSection 3. Provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently, any wine, beer, cider or liquor tasting that is provided\nfree of charge is exempt from the imposition of State sales and use\ntax. Additionally, any bottles, corks, caps, labels and other\npackaging that are used to package alcohol at that tasting are also\nsales tax exempt.\n\nHowever, wineries are currently granted this sales and use tax exempt\nstatus even if they provide a charge for such tasting; something that\nbreweries, cideries and distilleries are not. This bill seeks to\ncorrect this issue by amending the tax law to create uniformity across\nthe entire alcohol industry. With the continual growth of breweries\nand distilleries across New York, this bill would provide savings to\nnot only the industries that produce such beverages, but also the many\ncustomers that enjoy them.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-16: S.6556-A/A.8706-A Referred to Investigations and Government\nOperations/Held for Consideration in Ways and Means\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nMinimal\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the 90th day after it shall have become\na law.\n",
